{{!< default}}
{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     post.hbs — THE DISPATCHER, not a page.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Ghost renders every post through this one template, so the kind of page
     a post becomes is decided here, by its internal tag. Getting this wrong
     renders every post as the wrong kind of page — which is why it is second
     in abstract/ after the content model itself.

     THE RULE: one branch per collection, each delegating to exactly one
     partial in partials/post/, and NO markup in this file. The moment a
     branch grows its own layout, the two collections that share it start
     drifting.

     ══ ⚠ TWO VOCABULARIES, ON PURPOSE ══════════════════════════════════════
     Every training branch matches TWO tags. That is not indecision — it is
     the difference between what this repo decided and what the site is
     actually serving, and both are real:

       published content   canonical (tag-registry.md, decision 0004)
       ─────────────────   ─────────────────────────────────────────
       #training-section   #training-module     the module overview
       #training-content   #training-lesson     a lesson inside it

     Decision 0004 renamed `-content` to `-lesson` on the stated premise that
     "the theme has no content yet". That premise was false — 20 training
     posts were already published under the OLD names — so the rename was
     recorded but never carried out, and the theme has been dispatching on
     tags that ZERO live posts carry. Every training URL fell through to
     {{else}} and rendered as a blog article: no module head, no curriculum
     rail, and therefore no collapse control, because the markup the collapse
     script binds to was never emitted. The script was fine the whole time.

     Matching both is what makes the site correct NOW without a content
     migration, and still correct AFTER one. Ghost's {{#has}} treats a comma
     as OR. When the retag happens, delete the old names from these two
     branches — nothing else in the theme needs to know.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#post}}
    {{!-- Training BEFORE the generic branches: a training lesson is also a
         post with a primary tag, and the generic branch would claim it. --}}
    {{#has tag="#training-lesson, #training-content"}}
        {{> "post/training-lesson"}}
    {{else has tag="#training-module, #training-section"}}
        {{> "post/training-module"}}
    {{else has tag="#lesson"}}
        {{> "post/lesson"}}
    {{else has tag="#docs-page"}}
        {{> "post/docs"}}
    {{else}}
        {{> "post/article"}}
    {{/has}}
{{/post}}
